  "result": "**Tool calling, in one line:** the model can only write text, so it writes a request for an action, and a separate program carries that action out.\n\n**Analogy: an NFL offensive coordinator.**\n- The coordinator sits in the booth with a headset.\n- They can't touch the ball. They can only say \"run the slant on 2nd and 6.\"\n- The quarterback and the team run the play.\n- The result gets radioed back: \"Incomplete, 4 yards short.\"\n- The coordinator reads that and calls the next play. This repeats until the drive ends.\n\n**How it maps to your assistant:**\n- **Coordinator:** the model. It reads the situation and writes \"check the laser cutter calendar for Friday.\"\n- **Playbook:** the tool list. The coordinator can only call plays that are on it.\n- **Team on the field:** your program. It sees the request, actually runs the calendar lookup, and sends the result back as text.\n- **Radio report:** the tool result. The model reads it and decides what to do next.\n\n**Where it breaks down:**\n- A real coordinator remembers the whole game.\n- The model doesn't. Each time, your program hands it the whole conversation so far, including every earlier call and result.\n\n**Quick check:** in your own words, who actually touches the booking calendar, the model or your program?",
